      <data key="d8">26</data>
      <data key="d10">**Justification for New Evolutionary Task**
1) **Global Bottleneck Hypothesis (Objective Signal):** The global profile shows ENTER_DUNGEON at 0.11%, meaning the agent almost never descends to floor 1. Progress into the dungeon and beyond is gated by this action; all iron-tier crafting, dungeon mobs, and later realms require dungeon access. Improving ENTER_DUNGEON directly unlocks the next major progression tier.

2) **Parent Capability Frontier (What the parent proves):** The parent task shows the agent can reliably defeat a zombie on floor 0 (90.9% local SR) and has autonomously learned extensive early-game skills (wood/stone collection, crafting table, wooden/stone tools, basic survival). Therefore, we can safely compress away the prerequisites of crafting a wooden sword and surviving on the overworld. The parent frontier includes competent overworld navigation and basic combat; the next frontier is learning the surface-to-dungeon transition via DESCEND.

3) **Diagnosis: Local vs Global (Avoid local traps):** Local SR for DEFEAT_ZOMBIE was high; the agent did a lot of extraneous crafting that is already strong globally. The critical forward gap is the descent action itself. There is no conflict with local overfitting here—the agent simply needs exposure to a task where descending is required.

4) **Evolution Choice (Expand):** I will add one thin-slice forward dependency: the agent must now use the DESCEND action to reach floor 1. To keep the task learnable, I will remove all combat distractions (mob spawns disabled) and place the ladder‑down very close to the player start. This introduces only the new action, with no concurrent combat or resource pressure, making it likely to succeed within a short training window.

5) **Scaffolding &amp; Backtracking Avoidance:** The agent starts on floor 0 with a wooden sword (inherited from parent). All mob spawns are disabled, so no new threats appear. The ladder‑down is placed within 3–6 Manhattan distance of the player start, eliminating long exploration. No floor‑1 interaction is required; the episode terminates on success. This prevents backtracking and keeps the entire task on floor 0 with a single new action.

6) **Final Consistency Check:**
   - Trained Task Relevant Achievements: [DEFEAT_ZOMBIE]
   - New Task Relevant Achievements: [ENTER_DUNGEON]
   - New Task Completed Achievements: [MAKE_WOOD_SWORD]
   - “One-main-change” check: Only one new required achievement (ENTER_DUNGEON) added; environment changes only in mob‑spawn settings and ladder placement. YES.
   - Backtracking check: No need to revisit earlier floors; all prerequisites are provided on floor 0. YES.</data>
