      <data key="d8">49</data>
      <data key="d10">1) **Global Bottleneck Hypothesis (Objective Signal):**
   The primary global bottleneck is the ability to enter the Dungeon and defeat an Orc Soldier. The agent’s global `ENTER_DUNGEON` rate is only 78.64%, and `DEFEAT_ORC_SOLIDER` is a mere 18.72%, making this transition one of the weakest links in the progression toward more advanced realms. Improving the reliability of this early‐game sequence would have a direct positive impact on overall Craftax performance.

2) **Parent Capability Frontier (What the parent proves):**
   The parent task demonstrated that the agent can reliably fight and defeat a single Orc Soldier (local SR 78.23%) when placed directly on floor 1 with a stone sword already in hand and no other threats. The core combat capability against this enemy type is present under clean conditions. Prerequisites that can be compressed away for the next step are survival on the overworld and the ability to find the initial ladder (the agent already knows how to navigate to a ladder down when it is the explicit next goal).

3) **Diagnosis: Local vs Global (Avoid local traps):**
   Local performance on the trained task is high (78.23%), yet global performance on the same achievement is extremely low (18.72%). This indicates that the skill does not transfer because the full game requires the agent to **first** craft a suitable weapon and **then** descend – the isolated fighting skill is not integrated into the early‐game loop. The global profile shows that the individual skills of gathering wood/stone and crafting a stone sword are strong (all &gt;90%), so the missing link is the *chaining* of those actions with the descent and subsequent combat.

   The local task already included `ENTER_DUNGEON` as a completed achievement, so the agent never practiced the descent; globally, that skill is incomplete.

4) **Evolution Choice (Persist / Simplify / Expand / Vary):**
   **Expand** – I will add one forward dependency beyond the parent’s frontier: requiring the agent to craft a stone sword on the overworld and then intentionally descend to the Dungeon before fighting the Orc Soldier. This is a thin‐slice that combines already‐mastered individual skills into a short sequence that mirrors the real game’s opening. The change adds only a small amount of complexity (the sequence of two well‐known actions) and avoids bundling multiple fragile requirements.

5) **Scaffolding &amp; Backtracking Avoidance (Start-state design):**
   The agent starts on floor 0 with an empty inventory and no completed achievements. To remove unnecessary distractions, all hostile mob spawning (melee and ranged) is disabled on both the overworld and floor 1. This ensures the only combat obstacle is the one Orc Soldier placed on floor 1. Resources (trees, stone) are supplied by the default overworld generation, and the ladder down is pre‐opened (0 kills required). The map is further prepared by placing a single Orc Soldier near the dungeon’s entry ladder, guaranteeing the agent can find it soon after descent. Because the agent must craft the sword and then go down, the sequence is trained without the agent needing to “go back” to earlier floors.

6) **Final Consistency Check:**
   - Trained Task Relevant Achievements: `DEFEAT_ORC_SOLIDER`
   - New Task Relevant Achievements: `MAKE_STONE_SWORD`, `ENTER_DUNGEON`, `DEFEAT_ORC_SOLIDER`
   - New Task Completed Achievements: (none – the agent starts with nothing, all prerequisites must be actively performed)
   - “One-main-change” check: The single primary change is the addition of the make‐sword‐and‐descend sequence before combat. [YES]
   - Backtracking check: The task avoids requiring backtracking to earlier floors; everything needed is accessible on floor 0, and the descent is the intended next action. [YES]</data>
