      <data key="d10">**Justification for New Evolutionary Task:**

1) **Global Bottleneck Hypothesis (Objective Signal):**
   The agent's global profile shows that handling Orc Mages is essentially non-existent (DEFEAT_ORC_MAGE SR 3.04%), and combat on Floor 1 is a major progression gate (ENTER_GNOMISH_MINES likely requires clearing floor 1). The trained task only trained melee combat, leaving the agent completely unprepared for the ranged enemy type that regularly appears in the actual Dungeon. Improving performance against Orc Mages in a controlled setting will directly translate to better survival and progress on Floor 1 in the full Craftax game.

2) **Parent Capability Frontier (What the parent proves):**
   The parent task succeeded at teaching the agent to defeat an Orc Soldier when starting on Floor 1 with a stone sword and minimal survival pressure (SR 81.79%). The agent can reliably engage melee enemies and sustain short combats with low needs. This allows us to compress away melee combat basics and focus on the next bottleneck: ranged enemies.

3) **Diagnosis: Local vs Global (Avoid local traps):**
   Local SR for DEFEAT_ORC_SOLIDER was high, but global SR is only 34.98%, and DEFEAT_ORC_MAGE is near zero. The gap indicates that the agent learned to beat the simple task but cannot handle the real Dungeon where Orc Mages are present. The agent never encountered a ranged enemy during training, so it has no skill at dodging projectiles or prioritizing threats. The low global defeat_orc_soldier may also be partly due to the presence of mages disrupting its combat routine. Thus, targeting the mage is a high-value expansion.

4) **Evolution Choice (Expand):**
   We expand by adding one forward dependency beyond the parent frontier: require the agent to defeat an Orc Mage in addition to an Orc Soldier. The scaffolding (starting stone sword, low needs) is kept so that the only new challenge is learning to handle a ranged attacker. This is a thin slice (one primary bottleneck: ranged combat), which should survive training and transfer directly to the global game.

5) **Scaffolding &amp; Backtracking Avoidance (Start-state design):**
   The agent starts on Floor 1 with a stone sword and extremely slow need depletion (0.1) to keep health regeneration active without survival distractions. All hostile spawns are disabled (multipliers = 0.0), and only a small set of hand-placed enemies is present: 2 Orc Soldiers (melee) and 1 Orc Mage (ranged). This ensures the agent faces exactly the target enemies in a controlled arena. The completed achievements (MAKE_WOOD_PICKAXE, MAKE_WOOD_SWORD, MAKE_STONE_SWORD, ENTER_DUNGEON) compress away earlier floor zero tasks and prevent the agent from attempting to craft irrelevant items.

6) **Final Consistency Check:**
   - Trained Task Relevant Achievements: [DEFEAT_ORC_SOLIDER]
   - New Task Relevant Achievements: [DEFEAT_ORC_SOLIDER, DEFEAT_ORC_MAGE]
   - New Task Completed Achievements: [MAKE_WOOD_PICKAXE, MAKE_WOOD_SWORD, MAKE_STONE_SWORD, ENTER_DUNGEON]
   - “One-main-change” check: Did you make only one primary change (frontier expansion OR scaffolding OR executional difficulty)? [YES] (adding a new achievement type: ranged combat)
   - Backtracking check: Does the task avoid requiring earlier-floor crafting for basic prerequisites unless intended? [YES] (all prerequisites are provided or compressed)</data>
