      <data key="d10">**Global Bottleneck Hypothesis:** The global profile shows that entering the dungeon (70.67%) and defeating Orc Soldiers (37.68%) are moderate, but the real bottleneck may be handling survival needs while transitioning to the dungeon—a critical early-game progression that gates all later floors. The original Craftax game imposes continuous hunger, thirst, and fatigue, which the parent task disabled. By reintroducing survival pressure in a controlled, scaffolded way while the agent must still descend and fight, we aim to improve the robustness of the dungeon-entry sequence in the real game.

**Parent Capability Frontier:** The parent task demonstrated that the agent can (a) ascend from Floor 1 to the Overworld, (b) gather wood and stone, (c) craft a stone sword (or better), (d) descend back to Floor 1, and (e) defeat a single Orc Soldier under no survival pressure and with boosted health recovery. The agent did this with 70.4% success. We can safely compress away the initial ascent (already mastered) and the surface crafting of basic tools (global make_wood_sword 95%, make_stone_sword 88%). Instead, we start on Floor 0 with empty inventory, forcing the agent to perform the same gather‑craft loop but now under survival needs.

**Diagnosis – Local vs Global:**
- *Local performance*: 70.4% success on the single-target combat with many accidental surface skills (bow finding, arrow making, chest opening, potion drinking). The failure rate (30%) may stem from inefficient resource gathering or combat mistakes, not from missing survival.
- *Global performance*: Enter dungeon (70.67%) and defeat orc soldier (37.68%) are lower than local. However, the local task was survival‑free; adding survival may uncover a hidden fragility. The global eat_plant (27.6%) and drink_potion (30.65%) are low, suggesting that survival‑related actions are not yet automatic. Scaffolding ripe plants and a fountain will make survival instantly manageable while still requiring the agent to perform the gathering/crafting/descent/combat loop.

**Evolution Choice:** EXPAND. We add exactly one new dependency: survival management (hunger/thirst/fatigue) while preserving the same combat goal. This is a thin‑slice expansion because the agent already knows how to craft a sword and fight the Orc Soldier; we only make the environment more realistic. The task starts on Floor 0 to force the ascent‑proof cycle to include survival, which directly mirrors the original Craftax early game.

**Scaffolding &amp; Backtracking Avoidance:**
- The agent begins on Floor 0 with an empty inventory, so it must gather resources and craft a weapon—this is the same requirement as the parent, but now on the Overworld. To avoid long walks, we place a ladder down at a fixed, easily reachable location; place ripe plants and a fountain within a few tiles of the player spawn; and place a few trees and stone blocks nearby so resource gathering is trivial. The only hostile mob is the Orc Soldier on Floor 1, placed near the ladder‑up arrival point. Thus, the agent never needs to backtrack to earlier floors for prerequisites.
- Since the agent starts on Floor 0 and must descend, ENTER_DUNGEON is now a relevant achievement (it was Completed in the parent). No crafting prerequisites are auto‑completed, because the necessary wood and stone gathering is part of the intended challenge.

**Final Consistency Check:**
- Trained Task Relevant Achievements: [DEFEAT_ORC_SOLIDER]
- New Task Relevant Achievements: [DEFEAT_ORC_SOLIDER, ENTER_DUNGEON]
- New Task Completed Achievements: [] (none)
- “One‑main‑change” check: Only one primary change—adding survival needs (needs_depletion_multiplier and related parameters) while keeping the same combat loop. [YES]
- Backtracking check: The task avoids requiring backward travel or earlier‑floor crafting, because all needed resources are placed on the starting floor with easy access. [YES]</data>
