      <data key="d10">**Global Bottleneck Hypothesis (Objective Signal):**
The most critical bottleneck is the transition from the Dungeon (floor 1) to the Gnomish Mines (floor 2). Globally, the agent enters the dungeon reliably (89.89% SR) but only reaches the Gnomish Mines 5.60% of the time. This gap suggests that the agent can find the entrance but struggles with the sustained combat and resource management required to clear the dungeon. The global profile shows moderate combat skills against orcs but lacks any track record of handling survival needs during combat. Thus, improving "floor‑1 survival + combat" is the highest‑leverage intervention for global progression.

**Parent Capability Frontier (What the parent proves):**
The trained parent task demonstrates that, in a survival‑disabled environment, the agent can reliably kill orcs (98.34% defeat_orc_solider, 90.46% defeat_orc_mage), explore the dungeon, and achieve ENTER_GNOMISH_MINES with a local SR of 59.75%. The agent has mastered basic combat and loot utilisation (bows, chests, potions) under these stripped‑down conditions. This now allows us to compress away overworld crafting and focus entirely on the next layer: integrating survival needs into the same combat scenario.

**Diagnosis: Local vs Global (Avoid local traps):**
Local (trained task) SR for ENTER_GNOMISH_MINES is 59.75%, but global SR is only 5.6%. The agent shows strong local combat success but a drastic drop in the full game, where survival mechanics are active. Accidental local skills (e.g., collect_sapling, place_plant) are irrelevant artefacts of chest loot and can be ignored. Since the global bottleneck is clearly the absence of survival pressure, I will target the survival‑management skill as the new forward dependency, rather than attempting to fix any local weaknesses (which are not the limiting factor globally).

**Evolution Choice (Persist / Simplify / Expand / Vary):**
I choose **EXPAND**. The parent task proved the agent can fight in the dungeon; now I introduce **one** new dependency: managing hunger, thirst, and energy while fighting. This is a thin‑slice addition: keep the combat objective, setup, and kill count identical, but re‑enable survival needs (with a slightly reduced depletion multiplier to keep it learnable). This directly addresses the global gap and builds the competence needed to eventually survive the full dungeon in the original game.

**Scaffolding &amp; Backtracking Avoidance (Start‑state design):**
The agent starts on floor 1 with a stone sword and wood pickaxe (compressing the entire overworld crafting chain). It begins with full food, drink, and energy, avoiding an initial survival crisis. Survival pressure will accumulate over time, forcing the agent to hunt snails (passive mobs on this floor) and drink from fountains already present in the dungeon. No backtracking to floor 0 is required. The predefined inventory and completed achievements prevent the agent from trying to re‑craft basic tools, focusing training solely on the new survival+combat loop.

**Final Consistency Check:**
- Trained Task Relevant Achievements: `[ENTER_GNOMISH_MINES]`
- New Task Relevant Achievements: `[ENTER_GNOMISH_MINES]`
- New Task Completed Achievements: `[COLLECT_WOOD, PLACE_TABLE, MAKE_WOOD_PICKAXE, COLLECT_STONE, MAKE_STONE_SWORD, ENTER_DUNGEON]`
- “One‑main‑change” check: YES – only survival needs were added (with reduced intensity and passive spawns enabled for food).
- Backtracking check: YES – all needed resources are available on floor 1 (snails, fountains), so no travel to earlier floors is necessary.</data>
