      <data key="d8">97</data>
      <data key="d10">1) **Global Bottleneck Hypothesis (Objective Signal):**
   The ORIGINAL Craftax profile reveals a near-zero success rate for defeating Gnome Warriors (0.11%) despite the agent's ability to sometimes enter the Gnomish Mines (12.19%). Defeating gnomes is a critical combat gate that unlocks deeper dungeon progression (floors 2→3) and provides access to higher-tier resources. Improving gnome combat will directly lift global advancement.

2) **Parent Capability Frontier (What the parent proves):**
   The trained task demonstrates the agent can reliably defeat Orc Soldiers (98.99%) and Orc Mages (94.68%) when starting on Floor 1 with an iron sword, bow, arrows, and basic survival amenities. The agent can clear the dungeon floor with moderate consistency (53.42% ENTER_GNOMISH_MINES) under those conditions. Prerequisites such as iron sword crafting, bow acquisition, and dungeon navigation can be compressed via initial state, allowing training to focus on the next barrier: Floor 2 combat.

3) **Diagnosis: Local vs Global (Avoid local traps):**
   Locally, the agent shows high success on Orc combat and moderate success on descending; accidentally it learned to backtrack to the overworld and gather resources. Globally, ENTER_GNOMISH_MINES remains low (12.19%), but defeat gnome warrior is essentially zero. The local ENTER_GNOMISH_MINES rate (53%) is far above the global, so the agent has a foundational dungeon-clearing capability but fails to survive or fight on Floor 2 in the full game. This indicates the next high-value expansion is a thin-slice of gnome combat, not further polishing of dungeon entry.

4) **Evolution Choice (Expand):**
   We select Expand by introducing one forward dependency beyond the parent frontier: defeating a single Gnome Warrior. To make this learnable quickly, we compress Floor 1 entirely by starting on Floor 2 with gear a competent agent would have (iron sword, bow, torches, basic supplies) and we temporarily reduce gnome health/damage so the task survives long enough to yield skill transfer. This is a thin-slice with exactly one new capability (gnome melee combat) and no bundling of other fragile achievements.

5) **Scaffolding &amp; Backtracking Avoidance (Start-state design):**
   The agent starts on Floor 2 with an iron sword, bow, arrows, torches, and sufficient food/water (via placed cows and a water block) to avoid immediate survival pressure. The initial state implicitly satisfies ENTER_DUNGEON, ENTER_GNOMISH_MINES, and MAKE_IRON_SWORD (since the sword is provided). No backtracking to earlier floors is required. A single gnome warrior is placed near spawn with reduced health and damage to create a manageable combat encounter.

6) **Final Consistency Check:**
   - Trained Task Relevant Achievements: ENTER_GNOMISH_MINES
   - New Task Relevant Achievements: DEFEAT_GNOME_WARRIOR
   - New Task Completed Achievements: ENTER_DUNGEON, ENTER_GNOMISH_MINES, MAKE_IRON_SWORD
   - "One-main-change" check: Yes, this adds only gnome combat as the new skill, while compressing all prior requirements.
   - Backtracking check: Yes, the task requires no earlier-floor crafting; the agent is equipped for Floor 2 from the outset.</data>
