      <data key="d10">**Justification for New Evolutionary Task:** 

1) **Global Bottleneck Hypothesis:** The global profile shows that ENTER_GNOMISH_MINES is only 12.19% success, far below the local training score of 66%. This suggests that the agent can, in principle, execute the crafting chain and dungeon descent, but the combination of steps failing under global conditions is a major bottleneck. The immediate weak link is the low global rate of MAKE_IRON_SWORD (36.24%), which is critical for descending beyond the dungeon. Improving the agent’s ability to consistently craft the iron sword will raise the floor success rate of reaching the Gnomish Mines and unlock further progression, as later floors depend on having decent combat capability.

2) **Parent Capability Frontier:** The trained task demonstrates that the agent can, when given a pre‑placed crafting table and very slow survival needs, mine stone, coal, and iron, build a furnace, craft an iron sword, clear the dungeon, and descend to floor 2 – but only 66% of the time. The main point of fragility is gathering enough iron and coal; the collect_iron SR is 82.81% and make_iron_sword is only 57.92%. We can therefore compress away resource search randomness (which is a prerequisite to the crafting skill itself) by guaranteeing nearby iron and coal blocks. This allows the agent to focus on the core crafting, combat, and descent behaviours that are already partially present.

3) **Diagnosis: Local vs Global:**
   - Local: The agent fails to collect iron 17% of the time and to make an iron sword 42% of the time, despite almost always having the furnace and stone pickaxe ready. This strongly points to insufficient iron/coal availability rather than a failure to perform the crafting actions. All other basic skills are very high locally.
   - Global: MAKE_IRON_SWORD is only 36.24%, and collect_iron is 61.30%. This confirms that the randomness of ore placement is a persistent obstacle. Weakness here is genuine and worth targeting. Other early skills (place_table, collect_wood, etc.) are already high globally and can be ignored as a design focus. Therefore, increasing resource consistency is a high‑value “robustification” step that will directly feed into global progression.

4) **Evolution Choice:** PERSIST (with scaffolding). The parent task showed partial progress (66% main success) but the agent clearly struggles with the resource gathering step. Rather than pushing forward into harder survival or new floors, we should solidify the existing capability by adding a small amount of scaffolding – guaranteeing enough iron and coal within a short radius of the start. This ensures the agent can practice the full chain with high success, strengthening the neural pathways for the iron‑sword pipeline. Once this becomes reliable, a future task can remove the resource scaffold and/or reintroduce full survival pressure. This is a single primary change: increase ore availability.

5) **Scaffolding &amp; Backtracking Avoidance:** The agent starts on floor 0 with empty inventory, same as the parent. The only change is the placement of ore blocks near spawn, so no backtracking is required. No later‑floor context is needed, so inventory remains empty and no achievements are moved to Completed.

6) **Final Consistency Check:**
   - Trained Task Relevant Achievements: [ENTER_GNOMISH_MINES]
   - New Task Relevant Achievements: [ENTER_GNOMISH_MINES]
   - New Task Completed Achievements: []
   - “One‑main‑change” check: Only one primary change – added guaranteed iron/coal blocks near the start. Everything else (table pre‑placement, needs multiplier, level‑clear conditions) stays identical. [YES]
   - Backtracking check: The task does not require returning to earlier floors; all crafting happens on floor 0. [YES]</data>
