      <data key="d10">1) **Global Bottleneck Hypothesis (Objective Signal):**
   The global profile shows `ENTER_GNOMISH_MINES` at only 11.85%, while the trained task achieved 54.43% locally. The most critical missing ingredient is the ability to craft an iron sword from naturally generated resources (global `make_iron_sword`=48%, but that alone doesn't guarantee reaching the mines). Generalising iron sword formation in a realistic overworld will directly raise global `ENTER_GNOMISH_MINES`, unlocking further progression into floors 2+.

2) **Parent Capability Frontier (What the parent proves):**
   The parent task demonstrates the agent can reliably:
   • Fork iron sword when dense coal/iron clusters are manually placed nearby.
   • Navigate to the dungeon, defeat orcs, and descend (97% enter dungeon, 92% defeat orc soldier).
   These prerequisites can be compressed away via initial inventory (stone pickaxe, wood sword) and a pre‑placed crafting table, so training focuses on generalising ore location.

3) **Diagnosis: Local vs Global (Avoid local traps):**
   • Local performance: `ENTER_GNOMISH_MINES` SR = 54.43%, `make_iron_sword` = 51.48%. These are decent but heavily dependent on hand‑placed ores.
   • Global performance: `ENTER_GNOMISH_MINES` = 11.85%, `make_iron_sword` = 48%. The gap suggests overfitting to the easy ore layout; the iron sword crafting skill does not transfer to a natural overworld.
   • Other skills (wood/stone gathering, dungeon entry, orc combat) are robust globally, so the diagnosis is clear: the task’s artificial resource placement is the root cause.

4) **Evolution Choice (Persist / Simplify / Expand / Vary):**
   We choose **VARY**. The relevant goal stays exactly the same (`ENTER_GNOMISH_MINES`), but we **remove the hand‑placed coal and iron blocks**, forcing the agent to locate them via normal procedural generation. This is a single change to executional difficulty that should break overfitting and improve global competence, without introducing new achievements or unlearnable complexity.

5) **Scaffolding &amp; Backtracking Avoidance (Start‑state design):**
   • Start on floor 0 with `stone_pickaxe` and `wood_sword` to skip wood/stone tool crafting.
   • Pre‑place a crafting table directly adjacent to spawn ⇒ no need to harvest wood and craft a table first.
   • No special ore placement; all resources are from default world generation.
   • Survival needs at half rate (0.5) keeps the focus on the iron‑sword‑to‑dungeon pipeline.
   • Completed achievements mark wood/stone pickaxe, wood sword, and table placement as already satisfied.

6) **Final Consistency Check:**
   • Trained Task Relevant Achievements: [ENTER_GNOMISH_MINES]
   • New Task Relevant Achievements: [ENTER_GNOMISH_MINES]
   • New Task Completed Achievements: [MAKE_WOOD_PICKAXE, MAKE_STONE_PICKAXE, MAKE_WOOD_SWORD, PLACE_TABLE]
   • “One‑main‑change” check: YES (only removed manual ore placement).
   • Backtracking check: YES (starting inventory and pre‑placed table avoid any earlier‑floor crafting prerequisites).</data>
