      <data key="d6">True</data>
      <data key="d7">0.24376731301939059</data>
      <data key="d8">147</data>
      <data key="d10">1) Global Bottleneck Hypothesis: In the ORIGINAL Craftax profile, iron‑tool crafting (MAKE_IRON_PICKAXE 44.07%, MAKE_IRON_SWORD 58.21%) and subsequent dungeon descent (ENTER_GNOMISH_MINES 19.91%) are severely short of the agent’s average performance. These are clear progression gates: without iron tools the agent cannot mine diamonds, craft better gear, or survive deeper floors. Improving iron handling under more realistic game conditions should directly lift global advancement.

2) Parent Capability Frontier: The trained parent task demonstrated that, when given ample iron/coal placement, very slow survival drain (0.5×), fast health regeneration (2×), and a pre‑unlocked ladder, the agent can consistently craft iron pickaxe and sword and reach the Gnomish Mines (local success 54.6%). The forward frontier is therefore the ability to perform the same iron‑progression under standard survival pressure and with the natural combat gate on the Overworld.

3) Diagnosis: Local vs Global: Locally, the agent achieved high rates on most intermediate steps (collect_iron 93.42%, make_iron_pickaxe 80.26%, make_iron_sword 85.53%, enter_gnomish_mines 62.50%). Globally, those same skills are far weaker, and many baseline combat/survival steps are already solid (defeat_zombie 78.7%, defeat_skeleton 74%, collect_wood ~99%). This pattern indicates that the parent task’s easy‑mode settings allowed overfitting—the agent learned to solve the task in a comfort zone that does not transfer. The local weakness on `eat_plant` (41.45%) is irrelevant because global plant‑eating is similarly low and not critical. No single prerequisite skill is totally absent.

4) Evolution Choice: Vary (increase executional difficulty). The parent task shows high local but low global performance, suggesting overfitting to its generous scaffolding. To achieve robust global transfer, we must force the agent to handle standard survival depletion and a real combat requirement to leave the Overworld. I therefore keep the same core objectives (iron tools → dungeon → Gnomish Mines) but set needs and health regeneration to default (1.0) and require 8 hostiles killed on floor 0 to unlock the ladder. The ore placement remains moderately abundant (10 iron, 8 coal) so that iron‑gathering is not the primary failure mode, allowing the agent to focus on mastering the new pressure.

5) Scaffolding &amp; Backtracking Avoidance: The task starts at floor 0 with empty inventory, just like the original game, so no backtracking to earlier floors is required; the entire progression from wood to iron must be performed forward. No completed achievements are pre‑granted; the agent must earn all relevant steps.

6) Final Consistency Check:
- Trained Task Relevant Achievements: MAKE_IRON_PICKAXE, MAKE_IRON_SWORD, ENTER_DUNGEON, ENTER_GNOMISH_MINES
- New Task Relevant Achievements: (identical) MAKE_IRON_PICKAXE, MAKE_IRON_SWORD, ENTER_DUNGEON, ENTER_GNOMISH_MINES
- New Task Completed Achievements: (none)
- “One‑main‑change” check: YES – the primary change is removing the survival/combat scaffolding (needs, health recovery, and ladder unlock), which is a single theme of “make environment more realistic”.
- Backtracking check: YES – all prerequisites are obtainable on floor 0 and floor 1 without returning to an earlier floor.</data>
